findings.proposal:
A group kernel that, per group:
- evaluates the AO values once and forms rho with the GEMMs;
- calls libxc on the group's 224 points through libxc_itrf's per-thread _eval_xc;
- builds wv in C (GGA: 2·vsigma·grad rho, the weights, the 1/2 factors);
- accumulates nelec and exc per group, summed in group order;
- adds the group's Vxc from the same aobuf, using the deterministic schedule of xc-dot-detdyn.
Enable it only when ni.eval_xc_eff and ni.eval_xc are NumInt's own and xc is a libxc code, so custom functionals keep today's path.

In _partial_hess_ejk (rhf.py, uhf.py) and in the RSH long-range K loops of rks/uks partial_hess_elec, when atmlst is the default or sorted (Mole orders shells by atom):
- pass shls_slice=(shl0,shl1, 0,nbas, 0,kend, 0,nbas) to the ip1ip2 call;
- pass shls_slice=(shl0,shl1, 0,kend, 0,nbas, 0,nbas) to the ipvip1 call, where kend is the end shell of atom A (or the start of A's shells when combined with deriv-hess-ti);
- index the now shorter axis: vj1/vk1 become [9, nao_<=A, nao], and vk2 [9, nao_<=A, nA].
The dms and dm shapes do not change. For unsorted or partial atmlst, fall back to the full range.

When atmlst covers all atoms:
- skip the ipip1 call in _partial_hess_ejk (rhf/uhf);
- restrict the per-atom ip1ip2/ipvip1 calls to atoms strictly before A (atom 0 needs no call);
- after the loop, set ej[A,A] = -(sum over B != A of ej[A,B]), and the same for ek (per spin for UHF).
Keep e1 and all XC terms as they are: XC without grid response is not translationally invariant. Do the same for the long-range-K ipip1 and per-atom calls of RSH functionals (rks.py:76-107, uks.py:74-113), keeping the LR-K pair blocks apart from vxc_dm. Keep the old path for atmlst subsets.

In hessian/rhf.py hess_elec (and uhf.py, per spin):
- stack mo1 of all atoms as X [natm*3*nao, nocc]; dm1 = X.mocc^T and dme1 = (X*e_occ).mocc^T with two lib.dot calls, blocked over atoms if natm*3*nao^2 exceeds memory;
- h1 term for all pairs: 4*H[3A,nao^2].DM1[3B,nao^2]^T in one GEMM;
- s1 term per atom A, using only its rows because s1ao is nonzero only on A's rows and columns: 4*s1a[:,p0:p1].(dme1+dme1^T)[:,p0:p1];
- s1oo_A = mocc[p0:p1]^T.(s1a[:,p0:p1].mocc), plus its transpose;
- e1 term: 2*S1OO[3,nocc^2].E1[3B,nocc^2]^T.
Keep the lower-triangle-plus-transpose convention.

findings.evidence:
Code: hessian/rks.py:1290-1311 and 550-595 (GGA/MGGA) each run, per grid block:
- block_loop with deriv-2 AOs;
- eval_rho2 and eval_xc_eff(deriv=2);
- ao_dm0 = 4 _dot_ao_dm calls;
- per atom, _make_dR_rho1 and wv = einsum('xyg,sxg->syg'). wv is identical in both apart from MGGA's tau scale.
In _get_vxc_deriv2_dm, the t[] built by 21 _atom_pair_sum calls per block (rks.py:578-586) equals dR_rho1*[1, 1/2, 1/2, 1/2, (1)] bit for bit (t_equals_rho1.py, GGA and MGGA). uks.py:597-636 and 719-778 do the same per spin.
Profile, hess_caffeine_svp_b3lyp:
- eval_gto 0.842 s / 10 calls; _dot_ao_dm 1.186 s / 80;
- _make_dR_rho1 0.162 s self / 240, plus its einsums;
- _scale_ao 0.882 / 125; _dot_ao_ao 0.974 / 75; _make_dR_dao_w 0.581 / 10.
Per 67200-point caffeine block (xc_numpy_parts.py, real AO layout, 1 thread): _make_dR_rho1 x24 atoms 0.250 s, wv einsum 0.072 s, _atom_pair_sum 0.276 s. Each function runs 5 blocks.

findings.proposal:
Compute both terms in one block_loop pass, for example a shared helper called from partial_hess_elec that caches make_h1's vmat on hessobj (35 MB for caffeine). The pass does, once each:
- AO evaluation, eval_rho2, eval_xc_eff and ao_dm0;
- a stacked dR_rho1[natm,3,ncomp,ng] and wv;
- t = dR_rho1*scale;
- then _atom_pair_dot(wv,t) and _dot_sets_aow_ao(vmat, ao, wv).
Optional extras:
- move dR_rho1 into a C kernel (21 segmented products per block, all atoms at once);
- put v_ip (_gga_grad_sum_) and ipip (_make_dR_dao_w + _d1d2_dot_) on the It12 fused dot_terms. These now cost about 2.4 s in _scale_ao, _dot_ao_ao and _make_dR_dao_w; this part overlaps with the XC analyst.

Keep xs and ax as growing 2D arrays (in the in-core case), then:
- w = (axt.XS^T)/innerprod;
- x1 = axt - w.XS;
- h = XS.AX^T plus innerprod on the diagonal;
- g = XS.b^T;
- x = c^T.XS, all with lib.dot.
Keep _qr and the mask logic unchanged.

findings.expected_gain:
At 10 threads, starting from 0.220 s per call:
- (a) about 0.14 s per call, 1.57x as measured at 1 thread;
- (b) about 0.10-0.12 s per call. The ratio is 2.25x at 1 thread, but at 10 threads the 1-dm kernel is at the ERI streaming bound (It7 E: 0.056 s per pass), so two passes come to about 0.11-0.13 s;
- (c) about 0.07-0.08 s, one stream.

Savings on scf_caffeine+_svp_b3lyp_uks (25 cycles): about 1.5-2.0 s for (a), 2.3-3.0 s for (b) and about 3.5 s for (c), out of 21.16 s (7-17%). UHF and ROKS hybrids with in-core ERIs save the same per cycle.

findings.evidence:
Code (pyscf/df/df_jk.py:404-431): response dms carry no mo_coeff tag, so every dm is handled on its own:
- an AO2MOnr_e2_drv half transform with the full nao x nao dm, 2*naux*nao^3 flops;
- lib.dot(buf1.T, buf2), another 2*naux*nao^3 flops;
- lib.unpack_tril(eri1) is redone for every dm (line 429), although it is the same for all of them.

Measured at 1 thread (swarm/uks_df/df_k_response.py), 10 TDA-shaped dms, dm_k = Cvir X_k^T Cocc^T:
- benzene/SVP (naux 558): 68 ms per dm;
- naphthalene/SVP (naux 894): 394 ms per dm, at 53 GFLOP/s;
- a factored prototype on the same _cderi, K_k = sum_P (B_P L_k)(B_P Cocc)^T with B unpacked once per aux block: 17 and 102 ms per dm (4.0x and 3.9x). max|dK| is 9e-15 and 5e-15 with |K| up to 5.

Whole TDA runs (swarm/uks_df/df_tda_share.py), benzene DF-B3LYP, 5 states, 1 thread:
- RKS: 28.0 s, of which 8.2 s (29%) is DF get_jk for 104 response dms;
- UKS benzene+: 73.6 s, of which 22.0 s (30%) is for 280 spin dms.

Caffeine (naux 1242, nocc 51): the current cost is 7.4e10 flops per dm on SVP and 6.0e11 on TZVP; the factored form needs 0.21x and 0.10x of that. For comparison, the in-core conventional K of a TDA dm costs about 36 ms at 10 threads (It7 A, 20 dms in 0.71 s), against about 0.16-0.21 s for DF.

